Z||000007||Richard Parnell||11-01-1999||02:25 PM|||| I PUT THIS IN TECH DISCUSSIONS BUT I'LL PUT IT HERE TOO SO YOU DON'T MISS IT. <BR>RICH<BR>A few years ago I was having problems shifting into any gear. I decided it was the clutch needing replacement. It wasn't the clutch. Then I thought , must be the hydraulic system, it wasn't. It was the cluch release device that the slave piston pushes, it was the old version and had cracked . Replaced -shifting , no problem. Now to your question of how to do the clutch. I lossened the left front and rear motor/tranny mounts and the long bolts that go up and secure the body and cradle together. Pulled the left rear tire, wheel and strut asembly. and let everything drop. I separated the tranny from the motor, after pulling the shift linkage loose and disconnecting the hydraulic clutch slave assmbly from the motor. When first taking out two of the top (left and right) bolts connecting the tranny to motor, I inserted two (approx. 9 inch)bolts of the same thread size (with the thread only 1-2 inches) into the bolt holes. Then I removed the rest of the bolts holding motor and trans together. This put the weight on the long bolts and I could then slide the trans away from the motor and get into the cluch housing and replace the clutch, throwout, etc. It was tight and there are probably more items to remove that I don't recall right now. But it worked for me.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-01-1999||02:54 PM||||FieroShop,<P>what's so special about your clutch? and how does it compair against a Center Force unit? No one I know of has ever had a problem with them on anything, even with very big V8's on them.<P>The main problem I see in many replacement clutches, especially diaphram type, is cheap parts or reusing parts that shouldn't be. I don't know how many split diaphram springs I've seen on rebuilts. My little fords used to split a spring about every other year untill I found a better supplier.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fieroshop||11-01-1999||09:25 PM||||Theogre, I have had to replace several centrforce clutches due to the fact that the majority of these clutches have only three pins holding the two outer rings that hold the springs in place. Ours has four and cltches meant for V6's are specially constructed with six pins. Also our clutch is 100% Kevlar and we have aproximatly 2400 lbs of clamp load on our pressure plate. The fingers on the pressure plate are chromited for longer lifetime and our warranty should speak for itself. The warranty is if you got 50,000 miles on your last clutch then this one is warranted for 100,000. This clutch is not rebuilt but all new parts.||||reg||6
Z||000010||fiero56||11-01-1999||10:24 PM||||What kind of warranty do you give if you've got over 155K on the original clutch and can still roast 'em in the parking lot <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieroshop||11-02-1999||09:36 AM||||Fiero56, if you can prove that those are your own original miles on that clutch then you will get double the warranty on my clutch meaning that you will receive 310,000 mile warranty.<BR>One thing to remember is that this warranty is against defective parts. If this clutch fails during that time period for any reason other then racing and abuse of the clutch then I will replace it free of charge.<P>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 11-02-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 11-02-1999).]||||reg||7
